<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2024-10-22.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="000612"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#000612" label="[00:06:12]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: looks like bitmap fonts on the screen might not be copyrightable (at least in the US)</span></div><div class="line" id="001630"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#001630" label="[00:16:30]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well in arm/riscv/… world you could probably use serial instead of framebuffer-text, no?</span></div><div class="line" id="014145"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#014145" label="[01:41:45]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lanodan: same for most architectures; Although I doubt many people have a trusted VT100 terminal standing around.</span></div><div class="line" id="014351"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#014351" label="[01:43:51]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: does riscv bootstrap still require gcc 4.7? rather than 4.0.4?</span></div><div class="line" id="014356"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#014356" label="[01:43:56]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh sorry, 4.6?</span></div><div class="line" id="014417"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#014417" label="[01:44:17]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy: yes</span></div><div class="line" id="014423"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#014423" label="[01:44:23]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but that shouldn't be a problem</span></div><div class="line" id="014437"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#014437" label="[01:44:37]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, just thinking a good first step might be trying to strip 4.0.4 out</span></div><div class="line" id="014452"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#014452" label="[01:44:52]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yaeh, that would help in the future</span></div><div class="line" id="014505"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#014505" label="[01:45:05]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and might be nicer for x86 too</span></div><div class="line" id="014514"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#014514" label="[01:45:14]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">though what do we do then with GCC 4.7.4?</span></div><div class="line" id="014524"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#014524" label="[01:45:24]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's a bit pointless to build it with GCC 4.6</span></div><div class="line" id="014548"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#014548" label="[01:45:48]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">perhaps we just rebuild GCC 4.6 with C++...</span></div><div class="line" id="014555"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#014555" label="[01:45:55]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ideally i think remove 4.0.4 and 4.7.4 and replace with 4.6.x dual-pass build?</span></div><div class="line" id="014605"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#014605" label="[01:46:05]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">could be single pass before linux</span></div><div class="line" id="014617"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#014617" label="[01:46:17]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">sure, positioning of the builds doesn't erally matter</span></div><div class="line" id="014617"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#014617" label="[01:46:17]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, it's dual C pass anyway</span></div><div class="line" id="014626"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#014626" label="[01:46:26]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ah, true</span></div><div class="line" id="014629"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#014629" label="[01:46:29]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we need tcc-&gt;gcc -&gt; musl -&gt; gcc</span></div><div class="line" id="014632"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#014632" label="[01:46:32]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, triple pass then, lol</span></div><div class="line" id="014636"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#014636" label="[01:46:36]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">to get c++</span></div><div class="line" id="014636"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#014636" label="[01:46:36]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah</span></div><div class="line" id="020552"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#020552" label="[02:05:52]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: Well at least serial doesn't requires anything special to use multiple terminals (which can be relatively simple) and it's the kind of thing that allows to check logfiles, while VGA text… you need to trust your GPU.</span></div><div class="line" id="021229"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#021229" label="[02:12:29]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">That said I do have a Minitel here that I could use as an hardware terminal but the baud rate settings can be a bit annoying with modern boards using speeds like 115200 as IIRC it's 9600 at most.</span></div><div class="line" id="093529"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#093529" label="[09:35:29]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: I wonder about the analog bandwidth requirements for UART</span></div><div class="line" id="093547"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#093547" label="[09:35:47]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">As in, is it plausible to record UART traffic on tape?</span></div><div class="line" id="093600"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#093600" label="[09:36:00]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&amp; then play it back and have it decode correctly</span></div><div class="line" id="093710"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#093710" label="[09:37:10]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Would be a cheap &amp; easy solution to the problem of securely recording &amp; reviewing logs</span></div><div class="line" id="094918"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#094918" label="[09:49:18]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Hmmmmmmmmmm</span></div><div class="line" id="094920"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#094920" label="[09:49:20]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">very interesting idea</span></div><div class="line" id="101041"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#101041" label="[10:10:41]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">As some of you might recall, I intend to optionally make builder-hex0 &quot;verbose&quot; by having stage1 print stage2's source, and then stage2 would print out any sources it decodes in cleartext from the srcfs. (Compressed files would be printed upon decompression; and then once we can spawn an interactive shell, such &quot;firehose&quot; printing would stop, in</span></div><div class="line" id="101042"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#101042" label="[10:10:42]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">favor of interactive source code review.)</span></div><div class="line" id="101227"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#101227" label="[10:12:27]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">But to review such source code, a secure way of recording and replaying output would be needed. I initially thought of recording screen output on (possibly analog) video, but UART-on-tape might be even easier.</span></div><div class="line" id="101352"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#101352" label="[10:13:52]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I'm gravitating towards analog solutions here out of a concern that chips in a digital recorder could be subverted to hide backdoors in the logged code.</span></div><div class="line" id="220300"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#220300" label="[22:03:00]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">not sure if this question is appropriate here</span></div><div class="line" id="220313"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#220313" label="[22:03:13]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">tcc-related, asm-i386.c</span></div><div class="line" id="220504"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#220504" label="[22:05:04]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">for live-bootstrap you're patching musl-libc-1.1 with sigsetjmp.patch</span></div><div class="line" id="220623"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#220623" label="[22:06:23]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">this prevents a tcc_error(&quot;illegal displacement&quot;) when processing src/signal/i386/sigsetjmp.s:8 -&gt; jecxz 1f</span></div><div class="line" id="220800"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#220800" label="[22:08:00]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">anyway, i applied this patch, and noticed some statically binaries to segfault, backtrace with gdb showing this was triggered by musl-1.1 sigsetjmp() function</span></div><div class="line" id="220925"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#220925" label="[22:09:25]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ouch, hence, although your patch prevents a compile-time error, the change seems to cause a crash (couldn't diagnose this any further yet)</span></div><div class="line" id="221003"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#221003" label="[22:10:03]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">digging into tcc what's causing the compile-time error to address this instead is inconclusive too</span></div><div class="line" id="221116"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#221116" label="[22:11:16]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">to summarize, i can reproduce run-time crashes of binaries which seems related to the mentioned patch that's necessary currently</span></div><div class="line" id="221129"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#221129" label="[22:11:29]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i would welcome some suggestion</span></div><div class="line" id="221306"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#221306" label="[22:13:06]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">to reproduce this, for example, a statically linked bash or oksh reliably show the segfault</span></div><div class="line" id="223152"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#223152" label="[22:31:52]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hmm, no idea why would it cause a crash</span></div><div class="line" id="223209"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#223209" label="[22:32:09]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">is jecxz not the same thing as cmp and je?</span></div><div class="line" id="223229"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#223229" label="[22:32:29]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">JECXZ rel8 	Jump short if ECX register is 0</span></div><div class="line" id="223537"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#223537" label="[22:35:37]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so my understanding is that CMP ECX, 0 should check whether ECX=0 and if yes, set the flag in some register, then JE 1F will jump if flag is set</span></div><div class="line" id="223816"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#223816" label="[22:38:16]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">greetings stikonas, thanks for the reply</span></div><div class="line" id="223833"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#223833" label="[22:38:33]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, not much help here yet</span></div><div class="line" id="223837"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#223837" label="[22:38:37]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i have a suspicion, the value 0x1f could be interepreted as either short or int</span></div><div class="line" id="223842"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#223842" label="[22:38:42]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I just don't see what could conceivably cause a crash</span></div><div class="line" id="223846"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#223846" label="[22:38:46]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hmm</span></div><div class="line" id="223850"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#223850" label="[22:38:50]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">me neither, not yet</span></div><div class="line" id="223859"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#223859" label="[22:38:59]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but maybe you could help</span></div><div class="line" id="223919"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#223919" label="[22:39:19]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">to my knowledge, there is a some syntax prefix to explicitely label some value as short/int/long</span></div><div class="line" id="223941"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#223941" label="[22:39:41]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I don't even see yet, where tcc defines &quot;je&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="223948"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#223948" label="[22:39:48]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and i would like to try jecxz &lt;shortprefix&gt;1f</span></div><div class="line" id="223957"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#223957" label="[22:39:57]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but i don't know the syntax of this</span></div><div class="line" id="224132"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#224132" label="[22:41:32]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ok, it is in i386-tok...</span></div><div class="line" id="224141"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#224141" label="[22:41:41]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but  jecxz itself is not known</span></div><div class="line" id="224144"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#224144" label="[22:41:44]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and well, i don't know myself why a bash.static compiled/linked against musl-libc-1.1 with i386-tcc would crash in sigsetjump</span></div><div class="line" id="224150"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#224150" label="[22:41:50]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">moment ...</span></div><div class="line" id="224223"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#224223" label="[22:42:23]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ah, ok, maybe that's why tcc_error(&quot;illegal displacement&quot;) is emitted compile-time (without your patch)</span></div><div class="line" id="224245"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#224245" label="[22:42:45]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, yes, that's why I added the patch</span></div><div class="line" id="224251"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#224251" label="[22:42:51]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i386-asm.h:    DEF_ASM_OP1(jecxz, 0xe3, 0, 0, OPT_DISP8)</span></div><div class="line" id="224258"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#224258" label="[22:42:58]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh, let me check</span></div><div class="line" id="224304"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#224304" label="[22:43:04]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">which line is that?</span></div><div class="line" id="224316"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#224316" label="[22:43:16]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh I see it</span></div><div class="line" id="224333"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#224333" label="[22:43:33]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">was looking in i386-tok.h first</span></div><div class="line" id="224410"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#224410" label="[22:44:10]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">asm-i386.c:1072 tcc_error(&quot;invalid displacement&quot;);</span></div><div class="line" id="224434"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#224434" label="[22:44:34]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">which is related to the jump distance not being recognized as &quot;short&quot; i suspect</span></div><div class="line" id="224500"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#224500" label="[22:45:00]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hence the idea to prefix the value 0x1f as short explicitely, which some asm syntax exists for, which i don't recall which it was</span></div><div class="line" id="224523"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#224523" label="[22:45:23]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, syntax might exist, but  the question is whether tcc understands it</span></div><div class="line" id="224554"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#224554" label="[22:45:54]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so jecxz has OPT_DISP8,  /* Like OPT_ADDR, but only 8bit (short jumps) */</span></div><div class="line" id="224555"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#224555" label="[22:45:55]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i think it does, some of it; and it's just a first lucky trial and error</span></div><div class="line" id="224743"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#224743" label="[22:47:43]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: how about if you replace 1f with 1 ?</span></div><div class="line" id="224854"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#224854" label="[22:48:54]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">confusing, no clue, i might try</span></div><div class="line" id="225045"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#225045" label="[22:50:45]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i'll report findings</span></div><div class="line" id="225307"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#225307" label="[22:53:07]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the relevant issue is some tcc-compiled stuff segfaults in sigsetjmp</span></div><div class="line" id="225320"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#225320" label="[22:53:20]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">which may or may not be related to the patch</span></div><div class="line" id="225344"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#225344" label="[22:53:44]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i'll do some testing</span></div><div class="line" id="230528"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#230528" label="[23:05:28]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but how does it segfault there?</span></div><div class="line" id="230552"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#230552" label="[23:05:52]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i.e. what does gdb show</span></div><div class="line" id="230930"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#230930" label="[23:09:30]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: <a rel="nofollow" href="http://dpaste.com/DGSLRLK4Y">http://dpaste.com/DGSLRLK4Y</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="231026"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#231026" label="[23:10:26]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">no debug info in that function :(</span></div><div class="line" id="231042"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#231042" label="[23:10:42]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">perhaps add a breakpoint there</span></div><div class="line" id="231050"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#231050" label="[23:10:50]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and step through the assembly instructions</span></div><div class="line" id="231104"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#231104" label="[23:11:04]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&quot;layout asm&quot; followed by &quot;layout regs&quot; might help a bit too</span></div><div class="line" id="231501"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#231501" label="[23:15:01]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: i don't want to flood this channel</span></div><div class="line" id="231508"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#231508" label="[23:15:08]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but here's a few more bits and pieces</span></div><div class="line" id="231511"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#231511" label="[23:15:11]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, don't paste here</span></div><div class="line" id="231512"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#231512" label="[23:15:12]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">0x8085318 &lt;sigsetjmp+4&gt;         cmp    %ecx,0x0</span></div><div class="line" id="231513"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#231513" label="[23:15:13]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">use pastebin</span></div><div class="line" id="231531"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#231531" label="[23:15:31]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, but cmp itself shouldn't segfault</span></div><div class="line" id="231542"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#231542" label="[23:15:42]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that's where gdb halts, followed by the je instruction, right at the spot the patch applied</span></div><div class="line" id="231545"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#231545" label="[23:15:45]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I don't think it can ever cause segfault</span></div><div class="line" id="231548"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#231548" label="[23:15:48]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hmm</span></div><div class="line" id="231615"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#231615" label="[23:16:15]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I could see how je could jump somewhere illegal</span></div><div class="line" id="231621"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#231621" label="[23:16:21]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but not cmp</span></div><div class="line" id="231624"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#231624" label="[23:16:24]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hmm</span></div><div class="line" id="231816"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#231816" label="[23:18:16]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ok, thanks for the gdb hints; don't want to waste your time stikonas</span></div><div class="line" id="231825"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#231825" label="[23:18:25]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i'll do some testing</span></div><div class="line" id="231829"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#231829" label="[23:18:29]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah ok</span></div><br /></div></body></html>